\section{Introduction}
Dakka (ISO 639-3 dkk; Glottocode dakk1238) is an endangered Austronesian language of the Northern South Sulawesi group (Pitu Ulunna Salu), spoken in the Wonomulyo district of Polewali Mandar Regency, West Sulawesi, Indonesia. About 1,000 speakers were reported in 2010. Search found almost nothing beyond encyclopedic and classification entries.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes for Dakka were found in search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, Dakka language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dakka_language}
\item Glottolog entry: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/id/dakk1238.bigmap.html}
\item ANU repository, archaeological perspective on the diversification of South Sulawesi languages (context, not Dakka-specific): \url{https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/items/7bee88bf-5d8c-4249-abab-f4be3ed4bebc}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project profile of the Dakka people (mission-oriented, weak linguistic value): \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/11476}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None identified in search results.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
With a small, ageing speaker base and no known written materials, any curriculum work would begin with documentation and community-led orthography decisions. The classic descriptive source cited by Wikipedia is Friberg and Laskowske (1989) in NUSA 17; no URL for it was seen, so it is not listed.
\end{document}
